;; TEST-EXPECT: PASS
;; TEST-WITH[--hardware-profile=bmg --ir-target=spv]
;; SKIP-DEFAULT-PASS
;; TEST-HOIST[L0]: validate-l0-host-run
;; HOIST-HARDWARE-PROFILE: bmg
;; HOIST-EXPECT: BUFFER out: 0 256 2 3
;; SKIP-WITH[--differentiate]: "multiply is not differentiable by atomic-binop!'s VJP -- errors/11"
;;
;; ENDEAVOUR 175 -- atomic-binop! with an operator the HARDWARE DOES NOT HAVE.
;;
;; This is the construct's whole reason for existing.  Spec 35 accumulates with +, which is useful
;; for checking the retry path against a known total but is also exactly what atomic-add! already
;; does in one instruction.  MULTIPLICATION has no atomic anywhere in either target ISA -- there is
;; no atom.mul, no OpAtomicIMul -- so a CAS retry loop is the only way to do it at all.
;;
;; EIGHT THREADS, NOT SIXTY-FOUR, and the accumulator starts at 1.  A product grows exponentially:
;; 64 threads contributing 2.0 would be 2^64, past float precision and useless as an assertion.
;; Eight threads give 2^8 = 256, which is exact in float and unmistakable.  Contention is still
;; real -- eight threads on one location -- and spec 35 already covers the 64-way case.
;;
;; THE ACCUMULATOR IS out[1], NOT out[0], because the harness pre-fills each slot with its own
;; index and a product seeded at 0 stays 0 forever.  Slot 1 starts at 1, which is multiplication's
;; identity, so 1 * 2^8 = 256.  That slot choice is load-bearing and not cosmetic.
;;
;; WHAT THE UNTOUCHED SLOTS PROVE.  out[0] stays 0 and out[2..3] stay 2 3, so the construct wrote
;; exactly one location.  An implementation whose CAS pointer arithmetic was off by one would move
;; 256 into a neighbouring slot and the sum-based specs could not tell.
;;
;; ORDER DOES NOT MATTER HERE, which is why the answer is deterministic despite the threads racing:
;; multiplication is commutative and associative, so any interleaving of the eight updates gives
;; 256.  An operator without those properties would make this spec flaky rather than wrong, and
;; that is a property of the OPERATOR the caller chooses, not of the construct.

(def-type vec-t (vector float :address-space :global :align :compact))

;; No hardware atomic exists for this, on either backend.
(def-function ab-mul (a b)
  (declare #'(float float => float))
  (* a b))

(def-kernel binop_no_hw_atomic (&out out)
  (declare #'(&out vec-t => nil)
           (global-size :set-to 8)
           (local-size  :set-to 8))
  (atomic-binop! (~ out 1) #'ab-mul 2.0))
